  "worked_solution": "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}{(n+2)(n+4)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(3x)^{n+1}}{(n+3)(n+5)}\\cdot \\frac{(n+2)(n+4)}{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-3x\\,(n+2)(n+4)}{(n+3)(n+5)}| \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n+2)(n+4)}{(n+3)(n+5)} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n3|x|<1\\,\u21d2\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$",
